# Freshness and self-heal [SPEC:FL-FRESHNESS]

> Freshness is treated as a correctness property, not an operational nicety. The
> guarantee is not "the index is usually current". It is: **a stale corpus yields a loud
> stale marker, never a wrong answer** — and, because staleness is transient, usually
> yields a correct answer a few milliseconds later instead.

---

## [INV-FRESH] Freshness — the invariant behind every served answer

- part-of: [FL-FRESHNESS]
- status: draft
- importance: foundational

If the engine cannot prove that its index matches the corpus on disk, it refuses the
query and says why. It never answers from an index it cannot certify.

This is the invariant that makes every other claim in this corpus worth anything. A query
engine that occasionally answers from an old index is worse than no query engine: it is
correct often enough to be trusted and wrong often enough to mislead. A refusal is
actionable; a plausible wrong answer is not.

The refusal is structured, not an exception: it carries the corpus id, the index hash, the
live hash, and the reason. A consumer can tell "this corpus moved under me" from "this
corpus does not exist" without parsing an error string.

---

## [INV-CONTENT-ORACLE] The content hash is the oracle — never mtime

- part-of: [FL-FRESHNESS]
- depends-on: [INV-FRESH]
- see-also: [DEC-CONTENT-HASH-ORACLE]
- status: draft
- importance: foundational

Freshness is decided by comparing a hash over file **contents** against the hash the index
was stamped with. Modification time and file size are never a verdict.

Modification time is unreliable in exactly the cases that matter: a checkout, a `cp -p`, a
restore from backup, or a clock adjustment can move content without moving mtime, or move
mtime without moving content. Either error is fatal in a different direction — one serves
stale data, the other declares a fresh corpus stale and refuses to work.

mtime and size are still used, but only as a **hint** for *which* files to re-hash. The
content hash is then computed and compared, and only a genuine content change flips the
verdict. A `touch` with identical bytes therefore produces no false staleness.

---

## [INV-FAIL-CLOSED] Fail closed

- part-of: [FL-FRESHNESS]
- depends-on: [INV-FRESH]
- status: draft
- importance: high

Every failure mode resolves to refusal, never to a best-effort answer. An unreadable
corpus root, an unparseable file, a file rewritten during a rebuild, a rebuild that
completes but still does not certify fresh, or a corpus with no rebuild recipe at all: all
leave the served index untouched and return the stale marker with a diagnostic explaining
what was attempted.

Nothing propagates out of the repair path. A malformed corpus degrades the engine to
refusal; it cannot crash the process serving other corpora.

---

## [INV-DERIVED] Derived, never stored

- part-of: [FL-FRESHNESS]
- depends-on: [INV-FRESH]
- see-also: [DEC-DERIVED-NOT-STORED]
- status: draft
- importance: foundational

Diagrams are rendered from the graph at query time and written nowhere.

A diagram embedded in a document is a copy of a fact. Copies drift, and the drift is
undetectable without machinery to police it — a freshness marker on the diagram, a check
that re-derives it, a review step that compares it to the text. Every one of those is a
process that fails quietly.

Deriving the diagram from the graph removes the failure mode instead of policing it. There
is no second copy, so there is nothing to keep in sync. The same reasoning applies to any
other projection of the graph: a documentation site, an interface table, a dependency
list. If it can be derived on request, it should not be stored.

This inverts an earlier convention in which diagrams were hand-embedded in documents and
treated as static snapshots. Hand-embedded diagrams are now deprecated — see
[DEC-DERIVED-NOT-STORED].

---

## [INV-DETERMINISTIC] No inference anywhere on the query path

- part-of: [FL-FRESHNESS]
- see-also: [DEC-NO-LLM]
- status: draft
- importance: foundational

Parse, index, traverse, aggregate, lint and render contain zero model inference. The same
corpus and the same query produce byte-identical results, on any machine, in any order,
forever.

The contrast with retrieval-augmented approaches is the substantive design claim, not a
marketing one. An embedding index computes similarity at query time: it answers "what
looks related to this" with a ranked, probabilistic, model-version-dependent result. Its
recall depends on phrasing, and a relationship nobody happened to phrase similarly is not
found.

FactLattice moves the work to authoring time. The author — human-directed or an
Intelligence — **declares** that A depends on B. At query time the engine follows that
declaration. The answer to "what depends on B" is then complete and exact with respect to
what was declared, and its failure mode is visible: an undeclared relationship is missing
from the graph, and a reference to a node that does not exist is a lint error.

The two approaches answer different questions and the honest framing is that FactLattice
trades recall-on-undeclared-relationships for exactness and reproducibility. That trade is
right for specifications, requirements, decisions, and process flows — artifacts where
being confidently approximate is the failure — and wrong for open-ended discovery over an
unstructured document pile.

---

## [MECH-KERNEL] The incremental freshness kernel

- part-of: [FL-FRESHNESS]
- implements: [INV-CONTENT-ORACLE]
- see-also: [GATE-FRESH]
- status: draft
- importance: high

Validating freshness naively means re-hashing the whole corpus on every query — an
I/O cost proportional to corpus size, paid on every call, on the correctness path. At that
price the check gets sampled, cached, or turned off, and the guarantee evaporates.

The kernel keeps the guarantee and removes the price. Per-file content hashes are the
leaves; a combined root is maintained over them Merkle-style. On a change, only the
affected leaves are re-hashed and the root recombined; unchanged files are never re-read.
The query-path check becomes an O(1) comparison of the maintained root against the hash
the index was stamped with — no file I/O at all.

Three entry points maintain it: a full **seat** at startup or reconnect; a dependency-free
polling **watch** that uses mtime and size to decide which leaves to re-check; and the
periodic full re-verify specified in [MECH-BACKSTOP].

---

## [MECH-BACKSTOP] The periodic full re-verify

- part-of: [FL-FRESHNESS]
- depends-on: [MECH-KERNEL]
- implements: [INV-FRESH]
- status: draft
- importance: high

At a bounded interval, every leaf is re-hashed unconditionally and the root recomputed,
regardless of what the mtime hint said.

This exists to close one specific hole. The hint-driven fast path can miss a change whose
mtime and size are unchanged — `cp -p`, `touch -r`, a restore, a clock skew. Without a
backstop, a missed filesystem event would leave the engine serving a stale index while
believing it fresh, which violates [INV-FRESH] in the one way the fast path cannot detect
by itself.

The backstop turns "a missed event is a silent correctness failure" into "a missed event
is a bounded latency". The worst case is that the corpus is served as fresh for at most one
backstop interval past the change, and the interval is a deployment knob.

---

## [MECH-INCREMENTAL] Surgical re-index

- part-of: [FL-FRESHNESS]
- depends-on: [MECH-KERNEL]
- status: draft
- importance: high

When the corpus has moved, only the files whose content hash changed are re-parsed. The
per-file parse results are cached; each cache entry certifies the content hash of the bytes
it was parsed from, and a file that changes mid-parse invalidates the attempt rather than
producing a cache entry that cannot be attributed to any content state.

Every **cross-file** property is then recomputed in full from the cache: the node map,
first-definition-wins id collisions, the alias index, the dangling flag on every edge, all
per-layer adjacency, and all statistics. Only the parse is incremental; the resolution is
total.

That distinction is the determinism argument. A node deleted in one file must re-flag every
inbound edge from files that were never re-parsed. There is no targeted re-resolution
heuristic to get subtly wrong, because there is no targeted re-resolution: the fold is
re-run over everything, and it is cheap because it is in-memory list and dict work with no
I/O. The result is byte-identical to a cold rebuild of the same content.

---

## [MECH-SELFHEAL] Stale is transient, not terminal

- part-of: [FL-FRESHNESS]
- depends-on: [MECH-INCREMENTAL]
- implements: [INV-FAIL-CLOSED]
- see-also: [GATE-FRESH]
- status: draft
- importance: foundational

Detecting staleness and refusing is safe and useless on its own. A corpus that is written
and queried by the same agent would blind that agent to everything it had just written,
permanently, until the process restarted.

On detecting staleness the engine re-indexes the changed files synchronously, on the query
path, then re-runs the ordinary freshness check and serves only if that check now passes.
The repair is not trusted: the new content hash is published **last**, after nodes and
edges, so a concurrent reader can at worst observe new content with an old hash — verdict
stale, refusal — and can never observe old content with a new hash, which is the only
ordering that could serve stale data.

Repair is coalesced and storm-guarded. Concurrent callers queue on one lock per corpus and
re-check freshness under it, so the winner's rebuild satisfies all of them. Each attempt is
keyed to the live root hash it was attempted against, so the net rule is **at most one
rebuild per detected content change**, never one per query.

Background pre-warming is deliberately out of scope. It is a latency optimization on the
one component whose entire job is to never lie.

---

## [RISK-MISSED-EVENT] Risk — a missed filesystem event

- part-of: [FL-FRESHNESS]
- see-also: [MECH-BACKSTOP]
- status: draft

A change the watcher does not observe — because the hint did not move, because the
platform dropped an event, or because the watcher thread was starved — would otherwise
leave the engine confident and wrong. This is the residual risk the mtime hint introduces,
and it is retired by [MECH-BACKSTOP], not by trusting the watcher.

Stated plainly so a reader can test it: the guarantee is not "the engine sees every
change immediately". It is "the engine cannot believe a stale index is fresh for longer
than one backstop interval".

---

## [INV-FILE-IS-TRUTH] The file is the source of truth; filesystem-reconcile is the guaranteed path

- part-of: [FL-FRESHNESS]
- depends-on: [MECH-SELFHEAL]
- status: draft
- importance: foundational

The `.lts` file on disk is the single source of truth for what a corpus contains. The one
mechanism that makes a write visible to a query is the engine's own filesystem-reconcile:
the freshness gate compares against the file content and [MECH-SELFHEAL] re-indexes from
the file. A write is queryable on a LIVE process the moment it lands on disk, with no
restart.

Any OTHER path that pushes a change into an in-memory view — a writer that also refreshes
its own local index, a caller that pre-warms — is a best-effort OPTIMISATION over a
distinct view, never the path a query's correctness depends on. It follows that such a push
FAILING must degrade to the guaranteed file-reconcile, never to silence: if the write
reached disk, the engine will serve it fresh regardless of whether any push succeeded. A
design in which a failed in-process push leaves a write invisible has made an optimisation
load-bearing, which this invariant forbids.

---

## [RISK-STALE-CODE] Risk — a running server executing a stale CODE snapshot

- part-of: [FL-FRESHNESS]
- see-also: [MECH-BUILD-STAMP]
- see-also: [RISK-MISSED-EVENT]
- status: draft
- importance: high

Every guarantee above assumes the process is executing the CURRENT engine code. A
long-running MCP stdio server never reloads — it runs the python it imported at process
start. So when the engine library on disk is updated under it (as [MECH-SELFHEAL] itself
was, propagated to a server that had been running since before it existed), the process
keeps executing the OLD snapshot: the on-disk fix is inert and the live process is silently
broken. A manual bounce "fixes" it, which is the tell that the source was already correct
and the LIVE PROCESS is the stale artifact.

This is [RISK-MISSED-EVENT] one layer up — a missed CODE update rather than a missed FILE
change — and it is retired the same way: not by trusting that a running process is current,
but by a periodic re-verify that reconciles on divergence ([MECH-BUILD-STAMP]). It is
explicitly NOT resolved by prescribing a manual operator restart; a fix that lives only in
"someone remembers to bounce the server" is the recurrence this invariant closes.

---

## [MECH-BUILD-STAMP] The code-currency sentinel

- part-of: [FL-FRESHNESS]
- depends-on: [INV-FILE-IS-TRUTH]
- implements: [INV-FRESH]
- see-also: [RISK-STALE-CODE]
- status: draft
- importance: high

The server stamps its LOADED engine build — a content hash over the engine package's own
source bytes, mtime-immune, the same oracle discipline the corpus uses — and a sentinel
compares it periodically against the on-disk build. On divergence the running process is
executing code it never imported, and the sentinel RECONCILES by an automatic, invisible
relaunch (re-exec): the very bounce the operator used to perform by hand, performed by the
system. A stamp that cannot be read fails safe — it never manufactures a false divergence.

This makes the two silent-staleness classes symmetric and both closed: a stale FILE is
retired by [MECH-BACKSTOP]; a stale CODE snapshot is retired here. The reconcile STRATEGY is
a deployment knob (automatic relaunch by default; a loud refuse-and-alert for a deployment
that would rather not self-restart), but the detection is not optional — a server that
cannot tell it is running stale code cannot honour [INV-FRESH].

DETECTION IS ONLY HALF. This mechanism is stated in terms of what it NOTICES, and that is
exactly where it was under-specified: nothing here said what the RECONCILE owes. A reconcile
that detects perfectly and then leaves no serving process has not retired
[RISK-STALE-CODE] — it has converted a silent staleness into an outage, which is strictly
worse, because a stale answer is at least an answer. See [INV-RECONCILE-SERVES].

---

## [INV-RECONCILE-SERVES] Invariant — a reconcile leaves a LIVE successor on the SAME transport

- part-of: [FL-FRESHNESS]
- implements: [INV-FRESH]
- see-also: [MECH-BUILD-STAMP]
- see-also: [RISK-STALE-CODE]
- status: draft
- importance: high

The engine's primary deployment surface is a STDIO MCP PEER: a child process whose stdin and
stdout ARE the client's transport. On that surface a "relaunch" is not a daemon restart the
system can absorb — if the process ends, the transport ends with it, and every corpus query
in the interim degrades silently until a human notices and reconnects.

So the automatic relaunch of [MECH-BUILD-STAMP] carries three obligations, and each is a
verifiable count, not a hope:

  1. THE SUCCESSOR SERVES. After a reconcile the process is ALIVE and answering. `os.execv`
     is the correct primitive precisely because it replaces the image IN PLACE: same pid,
     same inherited stdio fds, so the client never observes an EOF. A reconcile that spawns
     a NEW process and lets the old one exit is a transport drop wearing a relaunch's name.

  2. THE SUCCESSOR RE-ENTERS THE SAME ENTRYPOINT. A relaunch must reconstruct the command
     that actually started this process, not a plausible-looking approximation of it. A
     server launched as `python -m pkg.mod` must be re-entered as `python -m pkg.mod`;
     re-running the module's FILE PATH is a different execution mode in which the module's
     own package-relative imports cannot resolve, and the process dies on import.

  3. NO REQUEST IS DROPPED ACROSS THE SEAM. The relaunch happens BETWEEN requests. A
     reconcile fired mid-dispatch discards a response that was never written, and the client
     waits out its timeout — a silent hang, the failure mode that is harder to diagnose than
     the drop it replaced.

THE PROOF OBLIGATION, because this invariant was violated by code whose test suite was
green: the reconcile may not be certified by a test that INJECTS a fake reconcile callback.
Detection and reconcile are different seams; spying the hook proves only that the hook is
called. The acceptance evidence must be a REAL process launched the way the deployment
launches it, over REAL pipes, observed still serving NEW code after a code change lands
under it. A test that shares the launch-shape blind spot certifies the wrong conclusion.

---

## Causal Graph

```
PRECONDITION_FOR: [MECH-KERNEL] -> [MECH-SELFHEAL]
ENABLES: [MECH-INCREMENTAL] -> [MECH-SELFHEAL]
ENABLES: [MECH-SELFHEAL] -> [INV-FILE-IS-TRUTH]
PREVENTS: [MECH-BACKSTOP] -> [RISK-MISSED-EVENT]
PREVENTS: [MECH-BUILD-STAMP] -> [RISK-STALE-CODE]
PRECONDITION_FOR: [INV-RECONCILE-SERVES] -> [MECH-BUILD-STAMP]
CAUSES: [RISK-MISSED-EVENT] -> [INV-FRESH]
CAUSES: [RISK-STALE-CODE] -> [INV-FRESH]
```

---

## [FLOW-QUERY] Flow — the query-path freshness decision

- part-of: [FL-FRESHNESS]
- see-also: [STEP-RECEIVE]
- see-also: [GATE-FRESH]
- status: draft
- rendered-by: `lts_flow(corpus="alpha", start_node="FLOW-QUERY", group_by="actor")`

The complete decision path a query takes through the gate. This flow is not described by a
stored diagram; the diagram is a query result over the steps below.

### [STEP-RECEIVE] Query arrives at the gate

- part-of: [FLOW-QUERY]
- actor: QUERY layer
- next-step: [STEP-COMPARE]

Any gated operation — find, node, traverse, capabilities, aggregate, flow — enters here.

### [STEP-COMPARE] Compare the maintained root against the index hash

- part-of: [FLOW-QUERY]
- actor: freshness gate
- condition: maintained root equals index hash?
- if-true: [STEP-SERVE]
- if-false: [STEP-HEALER]

An O(1) comparison of two content hashes. No file is read on this path.

### [STEP-HEALER] Is a self-healer configured?

- part-of: [FLOW-QUERY]
- actor: freshness gate
- condition: self-healer configured?
- if-true: [STEP-REINDEX]
- if-false: [STEP-REFUSE]

Healing requires a rebuild recipe on the partition. Without one, staleness is terminal for
this process and the refusal stands.

### [STEP-REINDEX] Re-parse only the files whose content hash moved

- part-of: [FLOW-QUERY]
- actor: self-healer
- next-step: [STEP-REVERIFY]

Surgical per [MECH-INCREMENTAL]. Coalesced and attempt-keyed, so concurrent queries against
the same changed corpus produce one rebuild.

### [STEP-REVERIFY] Re-run the ordinary freshness check

- part-of: [FLOW-QUERY]
- actor: freshness gate
- condition: fresh after rebuild?
- if-true: [STEP-SERVE]
- if-false: [STEP-REFUSE]

The rebuild is never trusted on its own report. The same check that failed is re-run.

### [STEP-SERVE] Serve the answer

- part-of: [FLOW-QUERY]
- actor: QUERY layer

The only path that returns data.

### [STEP-REFUSE] Return the stale marker

- part-of: [FLOW-QUERY]
- actor: QUERY layer

Carries corpus id, index hash, live hash, reason, and — if a repair was attempted — what
it did and why it did not restore freshness.

---

*FL-FRESHNESS — the correctness contract. Every guarantee here is enforced at the single
choke point specified in [GATE-FRESH].*
